hat how does the relationship  between China and Japan play into the  future and and how does it affect the  u.s. well in the big pitch if you think  about it East Asia's you know I've been  had a fantastic economic growth in the  last 70 years it's been an economic  success it's been a political failure  the Korean Civil War has never been  solved the Chinese civil wars never been  solved China and Japan have worse  relations now than they've had for  decades and if China and Japan have  conflict that automatically means that  the u.s. is in there as well because the  u.s. is japan's treaty allied sworn to  defend it and if they come to blows  that's the entire global economy goes  through these three countries and that's  a big deal you know you call it the  geopolitical version of Reservoir Dogs  which i think is a very interesting  visual all pointing guns at each other  and all blaming each other yeah I mean  they've all in different ways dependent  on each other threatening each other  that sounds odd to trade by each other  and trade with each other they're all  interdependent in a highly sort of  valuable way in many respects but also  violets increasingly volatile fashion  speaking of an issue we've been talking  about this morning and recently which is  North Korea and China's influence are  there circumstances in which China might  say to North Korea you've got to stop  this and we are part of the US effort to  get you stop it well the problem is  they've kind of been saying that for a  long time and North Korea's they're not  doing it no they haven't done it the  problem with China is China has enormous  economic leverage over North Korea but  it's the kind of leverage if they used  could come back against them you know if  North Korea collapsed if North Korea  fell into the arms of South Korea then  China would have a US ally on its border  and China's made the choice so far no  matter how crazy North Korea has got you  know keeping them there is better